<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- The stack: one cell on the home screen holding several cards, one
     shown at a time, paged sideways.

     An AdapterViewFlipper, after a StackView would not swipe on this
     launcher and a ListView scrolled like a list rather than settling on a
     card. Nothing that scrolls sideways is allowed in a widget at all:
     HorizontalScrollView is not a remote view, and the adapter-backed ones
     that are (ListView, GridView, StackView and the two flippers) are
     vertical or do not scroll. A flipper shows exactly one child, so it is
     paged by construction, and the slide animations give the sideways
     motion that a drag cannot.

     Every view in here is one the platform allows in a RemoteViews. That
     is a short list, and it does not include a plain View or a Space: a
     single one of those anywhere in this file and the launcher draws "an
     error occurred when loading widget" instead of the whole widget. The
     chevrons are placed by layout_gravity for that reason, rather than by
     a row with a spacer between them. They sit in the bottom corners, as
     square targets rather than strips down the full height, so they rest
     under a card's chart instead of across its figures.

     A tap between the chevrons opens the screen behind the card, and it
     is stack_tap that carries it rather than the flipper. The usual way
     for a collection is setPendingIntentTemplate on the adapter view plus
     a fill in per row, and that mechanism fires from
     AdapterView.performItemClick. AdapterViewFlipper never calls it: it
     inherits no touch handling worth the name, which is why it has two
     chevrons in the first place. So the tap is an ordinary click on a
     frame laid over the flipper, carrying the showing card's route, and
     this whole layout is rebuilt on every page turn anyway. The frame is
     declared before the chevrons so they stay on top of it.

     The two animations are animators, under res/animator, and that is not
     a detail: AdapterViewAnimator loads them with AnimatorInflater and
     casts to ObjectAnimator, so a tween from res/anim throws here and the
     launcher shows an error card in place of the widget.

     There is one of these, and there has to be. A second layout carrying
     the reverse animators was how the left arrow used to animate
     backwards, and sending it made the host inflate the widget again: the
     flipper came back with an adapter that had not loaded, and
     setDisplayedChild against an empty adapter clamps to zero, so every
     tap on the left arrow jumped to the first card. See
     animator/stack_page_in.xml. -->
<FrameLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent">

    <AdapterViewFlipper
        android:id="@+id/stack_view"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="match_parent"
        android:animateFirstView="false"
        android:loopViews="true"
        android:inAnimation="@animator/stack_page_in"
        android:outAnimation="@animator/stack_page_out" />

    <FrameLayout
        android:id="@+id/stack_tap"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="match_parent" />

    <ImageView
        android:id="@+id/stack_previous"
        android:layout_width="48dp"
        android:layout_height="48dp"
        android:layout_gravity="start|bottom"
        android:scaleType="center"
        android:alpha="0.9"
        android:contentDescription="@string/widget_stack_previous"
        android:src="@drawable/widget_stack_chevron_left" />

    <ImageView
        android:id="@+id/stack_next"
        android:layout_width="48dp"
        android:layout_height="48dp"
        android:layout_gravity="end|bottom"
        android:scaleType="center"
        android:alpha="0.9"
        android:contentDescription="@string/widget_stack_next"
        android:src="@drawable/widget_stack_chevron_right" />

    <!-- Gone, and with no text of its own, and both of those are load
         bearing.

         This file is also the stack's android:initialLayout, which is what
         the host inflates raw, with no adapter, no actions and nothing
         from this app at all, while a widget is waiting for its provider
         to send something. That is the state every stack is in for the first
         moments after a reboot, and with "Tap to choose what this stack
         holds" baked in here it was the state the user reported: a stack
         that had been set up for weeks asking to be set up again, every
         single restart, until the app was opened.

         So the sentence is set from code, by stackViews, which is the only
         place that knows whether this stack holds anything. Until then
         there is nothing to say and this says nothing.

         The visibility stops being ours the moment setEmptyView is applied:
         AdapterView owns an empty view's visibility and toggles it against
         the adapter's count. Do not set it from code as well: the two
         answers disagree for exactly one frame, and it is the frame
         somebody is looking at. -->
    <TextView
        android:id="@+id/stack_empty"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="match_parent"
        android:gravity="center"
        android:padding="16dp"
        android:textColor="#8A8A8E"
        android:textSize="13sp"
        android:visibility="gone" />
</FrameLayout>
